  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1200 AM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nFor Saturday, snow will fall across the mountains with snow \nlevels in the 8000's through the day, with values slowly \ndecreasing as the day progresses. Heaviest precip will occur \nSaturday morning across the eastern San Juan mountains, with \nthe precip decreasing later in the day. A total of 4 to 7 inches\nof snow will be likely above 9000 feet. \n\nIt will be cloudy over the entire region Saturday and it will be \nnoticeably cooler region-wide. It will become rather windy (south \nwinds) over the far eastern plains.  Showers will develop over the I-\n25 corridor by mid morning with the activity pushing onto the \neastern plains later in the day. The storm system is expected to \nwrap up somewhat as it moves over the plains, and this will allow \nfor some heavier precip to occur over the far southeast plains late \nin the day and into the evening hours. \n\nAs the storm wraps up, cooler air will spread southward on the \nbackside of the system, and we may see some light accumulations\nof snow across N El Paso county Saturday evening. \n\nSunday...any showers over the eastern plains will push into KS by \nlate morning. Otherwise it will dry out with sun returning to the \nregion. It will be cool Sunday with mainly temps around 50 over the \nplains and valleys. \n\nMonday...Sunny and mild with highs in the 50s plains/valleys.\n\nTuesday...Mild once again with 60s returning to the plains, mid 50s \nvalleys. Another strong Pacific trough will move towards the region. \nThis system is a fairly stout mid level trough but it will move over \nus as an open wave, and most of the forcing will stay to the north \nand west of us. Nonetheless, west to southwest mid level moist flow \nwill bring snows to mainly the Continental Divide region, and \nseveral inches of wind driven snow will be likely, especially \nTuesday night into early Wednesday. \n\nFor late next week, a rather very impressive trough is forecast to \nmove into the western United States. A large trough is forecast to \nmove in from the Pacific Northwest and then close off over the \ngreater central California area by late Friday. Moist southwest flow \nwill gradually develop over the 4 corners and snow will once again \ndevelop over the CONTDVD region by late week. This circulation is \nthen forecast to move slowly east-southeast with time.\n\nLooking at the Statistical DESI guidance, over 4 feet of snow is \nforecast for the Wolf Creek area during the next 10 days.",
